## sn21.11
sn21.11:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn21.11:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Mahākappina went to the Buddha. | T: Then the Venerable Mahakappina approached the Blessed One.
sn21.11:1.3 | S: The Buddha saw him coming off in the distance, | T: The Blessed One saw him coming in the distance
sn21.11:1.4 | S: and addressed the mendicants: | T: and addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn21.11:1.5 | S: “Mendicants, do you see that monk coming—white, thin, with a pointy nose?” | T: “Bhikkhus, do you see that bhikkhu coming, fair-skinned, thin, with a prominent nose?”
sn21.11:1.6 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, venerable sir.”
sn21.11:1.7 | S: “That mendicant is very mighty and powerful. | T: “That bhikkhu is of great spiritual power and might.
sn21.11:1.8 | S: It’s not easy to find an attainment that he has not already attained. | T: It is not easy to find an attainment which that bhikkhu has not already attained.
sn21.11:1.9 | S: And he has realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lives having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness.” | T: And he is one who, by realizing it for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life enters and dwells in that unsurpassed goal of the holy life for the sake of which clansmen rightly go forth from the household life into homelessness.”
sn21.11:2.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn21.11:2.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn21.11:3.1 | S: “The aristocrat is best among people | T: “The khattiya is the best among people
sn21.11:3.2 | S: who take clan as the standard. | T: For those whose standard is the clan,
sn21.11:3.3 | S: But one accomplished in knowledge and conduct | T: But one accomplished in knowledge and conduct
sn21.11:3.4 | S: is best among gods and humans. | T: Is best among devas and humans.
sn21.11:4.1 | S: The sun blazes by day, | T: “The sun shines by day,
sn21.11:4.2 | S: the moon radiates at night, | T: The moon glows at night,
sn21.11:4.3 | S: the aristocrat shines in armor, | T: The khattiya shines clad in armour,
sn21.11:4.4 | S: and the brahmin shines in absorption. | T: The meditative brahmin shines.
sn21.11:4.5 | S: But all day and all night, | T: But all the time, day and night,
sn21.11:4.6 | S: the Buddha blazes with glory.” | T: The Buddha shines with glory.”
sn21.11:4.7 | P: Ekādasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn21.1
sn21.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn21.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthi in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn21.1:1.3 | S: There Venerable Mahāmoggallāna addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Venerable Mahamoggallana addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn21.1:1.4 | S: “Reverends, mendicants!” | T: “Friends, bhikkhus!”
sn21.1:1.5 | S: “Reverend,” they replied. | T: “Friend!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn21.1:2.1 | S: Venerable Mahāmoggallāna said this: | T: The Venerable Mahamoggallana said this:
sn21.1:2.2 | S: “Just now, reverends, as I was in private retreat this thought came to mind: | T: “Here, friends, while I was alone in seclusion, a reflection arose in my mind thus:
sn21.1:2.3 | S: ‘They speak of this thing called “noble silence”. | T: ‘It is said, “noble silence, noble silence.”
sn21.1:2.4 | S: What then is this noble silence?’ | T: What now is noble silence?’
sn21.1:2.5 | S: It occurred to me: | T: “Then, friends, it occurred to me:
sn21.1:2.6 | S: ‘As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, a mendicant enters and remains in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: ‘Here, with the subsiding of thought and examination, a bhikkhu enters and dwells in the second jhana, which has internal confidence and unification of mind, is without thought and examination, and has rapture and happiness born of concentration.
sn21.1:2.7 | S: This is called noble silence.’ | T: This is called noble silence.’
sn21.1:2.8 | S: And so, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected were stilled, I was entering and remaining in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: “Then, friends, with the subsiding of thought and examination, I entered and dwelt in the second jhana, which … has rapture and happiness born of concentration.
sn21.1:2.9 | S: While I was in that meditation, perception and focus accompanied by placing the mind beset me. | T: While I dwelt therein, perception and attention accompanied by thought assailed me.
sn21.1:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha came up to me with his psychic power and said, | T: “Then, friends, the Blessed One came to me by means of spiritual power and said this:
sn21.1:3.2 | S: ‘Moggallāna, Moggallāna! Don’t neglect noble silence, brahmin! Settle your mind in noble silence; unify your mind and bring it to immersion in noble silence.’ | T: ‘Moggallana, Moggallana, do not be negligent regarding noble silence, brahmin. Steady your mind in noble silence, unify your mind in noble silence, concentrate your mind on noble silence.’
sn21.1:3.3 | S: And so, after some time, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected were stilled, I entered and remained in the second absorption … | T: Then, friends, on a later occasion, with the subsiding of thought and examination, I entered and dwelt in the second jhana, which has internal confidence and unification of mind, is without thought and examination, and has rapture and happiness born of concentration.
sn21.1:3.4 | S: So if anyone should be rightly called | T: “If, friends, one speaking rightly could say of anyone:
sn21.1:3.5 | S: a disciple who attained to great direct knowledge with help from the Teacher, it’s me.” | T: ‘He is a disciple who attained to greatness of direct knowledge with the assistance of the Teacher,’ it is of me that one could rightly say this.”
sn21.1:3.6 | P: ‘satthārā anuggahito sāvako mahābhiññataṁ patto’”ti. | T: (none)
sn21.1:3.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn21.2
sn21.2:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn21.2:1.2 | S: There Sāriputta addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Venerable Sāriputta addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn21.2:1.3 | S: “Reverends, mendicants!” | T: “Friends, bhikkhus!”
sn21.2:1.4 | S: “Reverend,” they replied. | T: “Friend!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn21.2:1.5 | S: Sāriputta said this: | T: The Venerable Sāriputta said this:
sn21.2:2.1 | S: “Just now, reverends, as I was in private retreat this thought came to mind: | T: “Here, friends, when I was alone in seclusion, a reflection arose in my mind thus:
sn21.2:2.2 | S: ‘Is there anything in the world whose decay and perishing would give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress in me?’ | T: ‘Is there anything in the world through the change and alteration of which sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair might arise in me?’
sn21.2:2.3 | S: It occurred to me: | T: Then it occurred to me:
sn21.2:2.4 | S: ‘There is nothing in the world whose decay and perishing would give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress in me.’” | T: ‘There is nothing in the world through the change and alteration of which sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair might arise in me.’”
sn21.2:3.1 | S: When he said this, Venerable Ānanda said to him, | T: When this was said, the Venerable Ānanda said to the Venerable Sāriputta:
sn21.2:3.2 | S: “Even if the Teacher were to decay and perish? Wouldn’t that give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress in you?” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, even if the Teacher himself were to undergo change and alteration, wouldn’t sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair arise in you?”
sn21.2:3.3 | S: “Even if the Teacher were to decay and perish, that wouldn’t give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress in me. Still, I would think: | T: “Friend, even if the Teacher himself were to undergo change and alteration, still sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair would not arise in me. However, it would occur to me:
sn21.2:3.4 | S: ‘Oh lord, the illustrious Teacher, so mighty and powerful, has vanished! | T: ‘The Teacher, so influential, so powerful and mighty, has passed away.
sn21.2:3.5 | S: If the Buddha was to remain for a long time, that would be for the welfare and happiness of the people, out of sympathy for the world, for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of gods and humans.’” | T: If the Blessed One had lived for a long time, that would have been for the welfare and happiness of the multitude, out of compassion for the world, for the good, welfare, and happiness of devas and humans.’”
sn21.2:3.6 | S: “That must be because Venerable Sāriputta has long ago totally eradicated I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit. | T: “It must be because I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit have been thoroughly uprooted in the Venerable Sāriputta for a long time that even if the Teacher himself were to undergo change and alteration,
sn21.2:3.7 | S: So even if the Teacher were to decay and perish, it wouldn’t give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress in him.” | T: still sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair would not arise in him.”
sn21.2:3.8 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn21.4
sn21.4:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn21.4:1.2 | S: Now at that time a certain junior monk, after his meal, on his return from almsround, entered his dwelling, where he adhered to passivity and silence. And he didn’t help the mendicants out when it was time to sew robes. | T: Now on that occasion a certain newly ordained bhikkhu, after returning from the alms round, would enter his dwelling after the meal and pass the time living at ease and keeping silent. He did not render service to the bhikkhus at the time of making robes.
sn21.4:1.3 | S: Then several mendicants went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: Then a number of bhikkhus approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and reported this matter to him.
sn21.4:1.4 | P: “idha, bhante, aññataro navo bhikkhu pacchābhattaṁ piṇḍapātapaṭikkanto vihāraṁ pavisitvā appossukko tuṇhībhūto saṅkasāyati, na bhikkhūnaṁ veyyāvaccaṁ karoti cīvarakārasamaye”ti. | T: (none)
sn21.4:2.1 | S: So the Buddha addressed one of the monks, | T: Then the Blessed One addressed a certain bhikkhu thus:
sn21.4:2.2 | S: “Please, monk, in my name tell that monk that the Teacher summons him.” | T: “Come, bhikkhu, tell that bhikkhu in my name that the Teacher calls him.”
sn21.4:2.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” that monk replied. He went to that monk and said to him, | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” that bhikkhu replied, and he went to that bhikkhu and told him:
sn21.4:2.4 | S: “Reverend, the Teacher summons you.” | T: “The Teacher calls you, friend.”
sn21.4:2.5 | S: “Yes, reverend,” that monk replied. He went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: “Yes, friend,” that bhikkhu replied, and he approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and sat down to one side. The Blessed One then said to him:
sn21.4:2.6 | S: “Is it really true, monk, that after your meal, on your return from almsround, you entered your dwelling, where you adhered to passivity and silence, and you didn’t help the mendicants out when it was time to sew robes?” | T: “Is it true, bhikkhu, that after returning from the alms round you enter your dwelling after the meal and pass the time living at ease and keeping silent, and you do not render service to the bhikkhus at the time of making robes?”
sn21.4:2.7 | S: “Sir, I am doing my own work.” | T: “I am doing my own duty, venerable sir.”
sn21.4:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha, knowing that monk’s train of thought, addressed the mendicants: | T: Then the Blessed One, having known with his own mind the reflection in that bhikkhu’s mind, addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn21.4:3.2 | S: “Mendicants, don’t complain about this monk. | T: “Bhikkhus, do not find fault with this bhikkhu.
sn21.4:3.3 | S: This monk gets the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when he wants, without trouble or difficulty. He has realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life, and lives having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness.” | T: This bhikkhu is one who gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhanas that constitute the higher mind and provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life. And he is one who, by realizing it for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life enters and dwells in that unsurpassed goal of the holy life for the sake of which clansmen rightly go forth from the household life into homelessness.”
sn21.4:4.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn21.4:4.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn21.4:5.1 | S: “Not by being slack, | T: “Not by means of slack endeavour,
sn21.4:5.2 | S: or with little strength | T: Not by means of feeble effort,
sn21.4:5.3 | S: may this be realized—extinguishment, | T: Is this Nibbāna to be achieved,
sn21.4:5.4 | S: the release from all suffering. | T: Release from all suffering.
sn21.4:6.1 | S: This young monk, | T: “This young bhikkhu by my side
sn21.4:6.2 | S: this best of men, | T: Is a supreme man indeed:
sn21.4:6.3 | S: bears his final body, | T: He carries about his final body,
sn21.4:6.4 | S: having vanquished Māra with his legions.” | T: Having conquered Mara and his mount.”
sn21.4:6.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn21.7
sn21.7:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn21.7:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Vesali in the Great Wood in the Hall with the Peaked Roof.
sn21.7:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Visākha, Pañcāli’s son, was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants in the assembly hall with a Dhamma talk. His words were polished, clear, articulate, expressing the meaning, comprehensive, and independent. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Visakha Pañcaliputta was instructing, exhorting, inspiring, and gladdening the bhikkhus in the assembly hall with a Dhamma talk, spoken with speech that was polished, clear, articulate, expressing well the meaning, comprehensive, unattached.
sn21.7:2.1 | S: Then in the late afternoon, the Buddha came out of retreat and went to the assembly hall. He sat down on the seat spread out, | T: Then, in the evening, the Blessed One emerged from seclusion and approached the assembly hall. He sat down in the appointed seat and
sn21.7:2.2 | S: and addressed the mendicants: | T: addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn21.7:2.3 | S: “Mendicants, who was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants in the assembly hall with a Dhamma talk?” | T: “Bhikkhus, who has been instructing, exhorting, inspiring, and gladdening the bhikkhus in the assembly hall with a Dhamma talk, spoken with speech that is polished, clear, articulate, expressing well the meaning, comprehensive, unattached?”
sn21.7:2.4 | S: “Sir, it was Venerable Visākha, Pañcāli’s son.” | T: “It was this Venerable Visakha Pañcaliputta, venerable sir.”
sn21.7:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to Visākha: | T: Then the Blessed One addressed the Venerable Visakha Pañcaliputta thus:
sn21.7:3.2 | S: “Good, good, Visākha! | T: “Good, good, Visakha!
sn21.7:3.3 | S: It’s good that you educate, encourage, fire up, and inspire the mendicants in the assembly hall with a Dhamma talk, with words that are polished, clear, articulate, expressing the meaning, comprehensive, and independent.” | T: It is good that you thus instruct the bhikkhus with a Dhamma talk.”
sn21.7:4.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said …
sn21.7:4.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: who further said this:
sn21.7:5.1 | S: “Though an astute person is mixed up with fools, | T: “When the wise man is in the midst of fools
sn21.7:5.2 | S: they don’t know unless he speaks. | T: They do not know him if he does not speak,
sn21.7:5.3 | S: But when he speaks they know, | T: But they know him when he speaks,
sn21.7:5.4 | S: he’s teaching the state free of death. | T: Pointing out the deathless state.
sn21.7:6.1 | S: He should speak and illustrate the teaching, | T: “He should speak and explain the Dhamma,
sn21.7:6.2 | S: holding up the banner of the seers. | T: He should raise high the seers’ banner.
sn21.7:6.3 | S: Words well spoken are the seers’ banner, | T: Well-spoken words are the seers’ banner:
sn21.7:6.4 | S: for the teaching is the banner of the seers.” | T: For the Dhamma is the banner of seers.”
sn21.7:6.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn21.8
sn21.8:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn21.8:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Nanda—the Buddha’s cousin on his mother’s side—dressed in nicely pressed and ironed robes, applied eyeshadow, and took a glazed bowl. He went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Nanda, the Blessed One’s maternal cousin, put on well-pressed and well-ironed robes, painted his eyes, took a glazed bowl, and approached the Blessed One. Having paid homage to the Blessed One, he sat down to one side, and the Blessed One said to him:
sn21.8:1.3 | S: “Nanda, as a gentleman who has gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness, it’s not appropriate for you to dress in nicely pressed and ironed robes, apply eyeshadow, and carry a glazed bowl. | T: “Nanda, this is not proper for you, a clansman who has gone forth out of faith from the household life into homelessness, that you wear well-pressed and well-ironed robes, paint your eyes, and carry a glazed bowl.
sn21.8:1.4 | S: It’s appropriate for you to stay in the wilderness, eat only almsfood, wear rag robes, and live without concern for sensual pleasures.” | T: This is proper for you, Nanda, a clansman who has gone forth out of faith from the household life into homelessness, that you be a forest dweller, an almsfood eater, a rag-robes wearer, and that you dwell indifferent to sensual pleasures.”
sn21.8:1.5 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said …
sn21.8:1.6 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: who further said this:
sn21.8:2.1 | S: “When will I see Nanda | T: “When shall I see Nanda as a forest dweller,
sn21.8:2.2 | S: in the wilderness, wearing rag robes, | T: Wearing robes stitched from rags,
sn21.8:2.3 | S: feeding on scraps offered by strangers, | T: Subsisting on the scraps of strangers,
sn21.8:2.4 | S: unconcerned for sensual pleasures?” | T: Indifferent towards sensual pleasures?”
sn21.8:3.1 | S: Then some time later Venerable Nanda stayed in the wilderness, ate only almsfood, wore rag robes, and lived without concern for sensual pleasures. | T: Then, some time later, the Venerable Nanda became a forest dweller, an almsfood eater, a rag-robes wearer, and he dwelt indifferent to sensual pleasures.
sn21.8:3.2 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.101
sn22.101:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.101:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I say that the ending of defilements is for one who knows and sees, not for one who does not know or see. | T: “Bhikkhus, I say that the destruction of the taints is for one who knows and sees, not for one who does not know and does not see.
sn22.101:1.3 | S: For one who knows and sees what? | T: For one who knows what, who sees what, does the destruction of the taints come about?
sn22.101:1.4 | S: ‘Such is form, such is the origin of form, such is the disappearance of form. | T: ‘Such is form, such its origin, such its passing away;
sn22.101:1.5 | S: Such is feeling … | T: such is feeling …
sn22.101:1.6 | S: Such is perception … | T: such is perception …
sn22.101:1.7 | S: Such are choices … | T: such are volitional formations …
sn22.101:1.8 | S: Such is consciousness, such is the origin of consciousness, such is the disappearance of consciousness.’ | T: such is consciousness, such its origin, such its passing away’:
sn22.101:1.9 | S: The ending of the defilements is for one who knows and sees this. | T: it is for one who knows thus, for one who sees thus, that the destruction of the taints comes about.
sn22.101:2.1 | S: When a mendicant is not committed to development, they might wish: | T: “Bhikkhus, when a bhikkhu does not dwell devoted to development, even though such a wish as this might arise in him:
sn22.101:2.2 | S: ‘If only my mind were freed from the defilements by not grasping!’ Even so, their mind is not freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: ‘Oh, that my mind might be liberated from the taints by nonclinging!’ yet his mind is not liberated from the taints by nonclinging.
sn22.101:2.3 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn22.101:2.4 | S: You should say: ‘It’s because they’re undeveloped.’ | T: It should be said: because of nondevelopment.
sn22.101:2.5 | S: Undeveloped in what? | T: Because of not developing what?
sn22.101:2.6 | S: Undeveloped in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the noble eightfold path. | T: Because of not developing the four establishments of mindfulness … the four right strivings … the four bases for spiritual power … the five spiritual faculties … the five powers … the seven factors of enlightenment … the Noble Eightfold Path.
sn22.101:3.1 | S: Suppose there was a chicken with eight or ten or twelve eggs. | T: “Suppose, bhikkhus there was a hen with eight, ten, or twelve eggs
sn22.101:3.2 | S: But she had not properly sat on them to keep them warm and incubated. | T: that she had not covered, incubated, and nurtured properly.
sn22.101:3.3 | S: That chicken might wish: | T: Even though such a wish as this might arise in her:
sn22.101:3.4 | S: ‘If only my chicks could break out of the eggshell with their claws and beak and hatch safely!’ | T: ‘Oh, that my chicks might pierce their shells with the points of their claws and beaks and hatch safely!’
sn22.101:3.5 | S: But they can’t break out and hatch safely. | T: yet the chicks are incapable of piercing their shells with the points of their claws and beaks and hatching safely.
